WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.683
Ownership becomes visible when designing
functions.

2
00:00:02.763 --> 00:00:05.943
A function that takes a value by ownership
is saying it needs responsibility for

3
00:00:06.023 --> 00:00:06.498
that value.

4
00:00:06.578 --> 00:00:09.046
It may store the value, transform it into
another owned value,

5
00:00:09.126 --> 00:00:11.784
send it to another component, or consume
it as part of an operation.

6
00:00:11.864 --> 00:00:14.759
The caller should not expect to keep using
the original owner afterward.

7
00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:17.402
A function that takes a reference is
making a different promise.

8
00:00:17.482 --> 00:00:18.763
It only needs temporary access.

9
00:00:18.843 --> 00:00:20.932
A shared reference says read-only access
is enough.

10
00:00:21.012 --> 00:00:24.239
A mutable reference says the function
needs to change existing state but does not

11
00:00:24.319 --> 00:00:25.411
need to own it permanently.

12
00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:28.671
These signatures are contracts that help
callers and reviewers understand the

13
00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:29.463
function's role.

14
00:00:29.543 --> 00:00:30.872
Structs also express ownership.

15
00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:32.043
A struct can own its fields,

16
00:00:32.123 --> 00:00:35.019
which is common when the value should be
self-contained and easy to move.

17
00:00:35.099 --> 00:00:36.807
A struct can borrow fields with lifetimes,

18
00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:39.735
which can be efficient but requires
careful relationship management.

19
00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:43.042
A struct can also hold smart pointers or
shared ownership handles when the design

20
00:00:43.122 --> 00:00:43.834
truly needs them.

21
00:00:43.914 --> 00:00:46.098
Enums can carry owned data through state
transitions.

22
00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.930
A state enum might own a pending request,
a validated configuration,

23
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:49.912
or a completed result.

24
00:00:49.992 --> 00:00:53.409
This can make impossible states harder to
represent because each variant carries the

25
00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:54.818
data appropriate for that state.

26
00:00:54.898 --> 00:00:58.078
Ownership becomes part of the domain model
rather than a separate housekeeping

27
00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:58.538
concern.

28
00:00:58.618 --> 00:01:01.988
Constructors and builder patterns can make
ownership safer by validating input and

29
00:01:02.068 --> 00:01:04.536
assembling owned state before the rest of
the program uses it.

30
00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:07.179
Instead of spreading partial assumptions
across the codebase,

31
00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:09.679
a type can own its validated fields and
expose clear methods.

32
00:01:09.759 --> 00:01:12.180
Good Rust APIs use ownership choices as
part of the contract.
